    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p20-q14-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find $A$ and $B$ such that $A(m+1)^2+B(m-1)^2=4m$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$A=1$, $B=-1$.",
          "worked": "Equating coefficients of $m^2$ and $m$ gives\n$$\\begin{align*}A+B&=0\\\\2A-2B&=4\\\\A-B&=2.\\end{align*}$$\nAdding the first and third equations gives $2A=2$, so $A=1$ and $B=-1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 40
      },
      {
        "id": "p20-q14-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence find the sum of the first eleven multiples of 4.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$264$.\n\nUse $4m=(m+1)^2-(m-1)^2$ and cancel the repeated squares.",
          "worked": "From part a, $4m=(m+1)^2-(m-1)^2$. Substituting $m=1,2,\\ldots,11$ and adding gives\n$$\\begin{align*}4+8+\\cdots+44&=(2^2-0^2)+(3^2-1^2)+\\cdots+(12^2-10^2)\\\\4+8+\\cdots+44&=11^2+12^2-0^2-1^2\\\\4+8+\\cdots+44&=121+144-1\\\\4+8+\\cdots+44&=264.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      }
    ]
  },
